A||||20||AutoXdbdragSE||WHY?!?||||5||
Z||000000||AutoXdbdragSE||11-21-2001||12:52 AM||||Why is it that everyone rags on the 84-87 suspension.. it's not that bad.. i've yet to ride in an 88 fiero so i'm not sure how "MUCH BETTER" they are. but i have ridden in some seriously good handling cars(4th & 5th Gen VETTES, Trans ams, RX-7's, 240sx, porsche 914's, MG midget, MGA, numerous british sports cars, BMW's of all types.. etc..) and my 84 haddles as well as EVERY one of them.  it just sticks to the road, it's not heavy, it's not clumbsy, it modulates understeer/oversteer very well.  And for those of you that doubt me in autocross i can keep up with almost anything, at my last event i was running dead even on street tires with the neon guys on racing suspensions/tires.  So anyway, what are the skidpad numbers on this meca of a suspension that is the 88?  Why is it that you all need to constantly bring light to the poor handling caracteristics that don't exist in our chevette suspensioned cars?..  .  . um.. ok rant over i guess||||reg||5
Z||000001||SKIDMARK||11-21-2001||01:02 AM||||I agree. I have driven both and didn't see an obvious difference in the handling and ride. Now the brakes, that's a different story, at least for my car. The 88's brakes were 50% better. It might just be my car though.<P>------------------<BR>White '85 4cyl coupe<BR>Black '86 v-6 SE<BR>'81 CB900F Super Sport||||reg||1
Z||000002||Master_Sushi||11-21-2001||01:18 AM||||I have owned both an 88 coupe, and I am on a 84se now. The 88 by far handels way better, the whole car infact seems more solid. Both have/had 16 inch wheels with simmilar tires. <P>In really hard cornering the front of my 84 just seems to push into the curve.  I can almost never get the backend to come out. When I would push my 88 really hard the whole car seems to slide evenly.<P>When hiting bumps on the road in an 88 it is way more stable, at high speeds much beter also. I beleave the 88s are just a more modern and updated/upgraded setup. Also the brakes are WAY better.<P>Stock for stock any 88 will outhandel the older setups. But pre 88s can be easily set up and moded to handel better then an 88. Its all about money!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Standard||11-21-2001||01:25 AM||||Stock for stock, the '88 is better.. by design.  When you start modding either, it just comes down to how much money you put into it.<P>I can't directly compare.. I went from an 86 GT with new KYB's (everything else original with 160,000 miles except the ball joints) to a lowered 88 with all brand new stuff, full poly, and a rear sway bar.  Brakes.. hehe.. well, I don't know if I'll be able to go back to the 84-87 brakes...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic_full.jpg">           <BR><FONT COLOR="red">1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed</FONT><BR><FONT SIZE="1"><FONT COLOR="white">1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="blue">AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus</FONT></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Standard (edited 11-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||AutoXdbdragSE||11-21-2001||01:28 AM||||I'll admit the brakes leave lots to be desired but otherwise road holding, and manner. i don't find a problem witht he 84-87 setup||||reg||1
Z||000005||LZeitgeist||11-21-2001||02:23 AM||||I think it's because the '88's finally got the suspension that the designers intended to put on the cars back in '84, until the beancounters told them to cut costs and use existing parts from other cars, like Chevettes, etc.<P>There's something comforting knowing that the entire car is completely as the original designers intended, with nothing held back. However, it's also a pain in the ass getting parts and service for a car that was only offered for a single model year, too, so it's a premium that comes with a price.<P>Just my two cents. You get what you pay for. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Patrick W. Heinske  --  LZeitgeist@aol.com<BR>1988 Red Fiero Formula<BR>- 1st Place - Stock Coupe - FOCOSEVA 2000<BR>- 3rd Place - Stock Formula - FOCOA Nat'l 2001<BR>- Class Winner - 1982 to Present - 2001 Tarheel Tigers All-Pontiac Show||||reg||1
Z||000006||G-Nasty||11-21-2001||02:57 AM||||88's have that awful sensitive steering rack where you feel every pebble & insect on the road. I finished putting a fierostore rear sway bar on my 87GT and found it was made by KONI. I have KONI shocks w both BEL-TEC Spindles and ST springs upfront and Bilstein rear struts on ST rear springs. I upgraded to Beretta vented brakes up front were all rotors are cross-drilled. MOOG dampner upfront. ALL fieros could'ved should'ved used a REAL double wishbone suspension like newer RX7s and SUPRAs and 11" 2 or 4 piston brakes. My car handles allright...<BR>Its up to us to do these cars justice.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||G-Nasty||11-21-2001||03:10 AM||||I also forgot to mention my ride has 16" 225 ZR 45 yokamomma AV520's upfront and  17" 235 45's in da back jack- These are beautiful and super rare super expensive O.Z. corsa forged austrian rims...they have put these on the newer lotus V8 turbos. I bought these way back and come as a direct bolt on's.<BR>I cant tell how many people have complemented me. The car is glass smooth even up to speeds over 110 mph.Its too bad O.Z. quit making these 3 yrs ago. These rims and tires cost more than most used cars-<BR>OUT&gt;<P>   ||||reg||1
Z||000008||cadero2dmax||11-21-2001||07:44 AM||||I would suggest that if you are interested in handling, you join the:<BR>      FieroRacingList@yahoogroups.com.<BR>These guys compete in their Fieros, and on the track is where you will be able to best tell any subtle difference in any suspension.<P>Personally, I like the early chassis front suspension, it seems more responsive, has a quicker turn-in, and gives better feedback. Both of my current (early model) Fieros have the '88 cradle and rear suspension, however.<P>This combo, early chassis with late cradle, is the best handling Fiero you will ever drive. The bump steer is gone, it is smooth and responsive, and easier to drive.<P>As has been addressed, the brakes are better on the '88. I have one of my cars with the GA conversion on the front, the other has the stock front brakes with a racing pad, Motul fluid, braided lines, etc. Don't notice that much difference between the two. If you take care of the stock brakes, they do just fine. And there is quite a few less pounds of unsprung weight at each corner than there is with the GA, LeBaron, or Camaro brake upgrades (which makes the car handle a lot better, too).<P>G  ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Raydar||11-21-2001||10:36 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by G-Nasty:<BR><B>88's have that awful sensitive steering rack where you feel every pebble & insect on the road</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I have an 88 coupe and an 88 Formula. I used to have an 85 GT, that was purchased new.<BR>Both of my well worn 88s handle better than the 85 did when it was new. <P>Speaking of steering wheel feedback...<BR>The main reason I traded the GT was when I drove from Atlanta to Savannah on I-75, while they were doing construction, the grooved pavement tried to twist the wheel out of my hands the entire time I drove on it. I know that tires have a lot to do with it, but the car shouldn't fight you every step of the way. That's the reason the 84-87s all had steering dampers, but the 88s didn't. The 88 was a much better design and <I>didn't need</I> damping. <BR>I know many will disagree with me, but that's my take on it. <BR>BTW, In retrospect, that still wouldn't discourage me from buying a nice 85-87 GT or SE. Now that mods are available.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <P><P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000010||fldevel||11-21-2001||11:53 AM||||i have an  85 GT and an 88 formula and to me both cars handle only as good as i drive them..either car will bite you if you push them too hard ..the 85 has a little front end push when i get on it through corners and the 88 tends to track a little better ..yet i have to admitt the 88 breaks are by far superior ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Fformula88||11-21-2001||12:14 PM||||Between my 88 Formula, and my fathers 85 SE 2M6 (it has the WS6 suspension package) I'd have to say my Formula handles better, but not so much as to discourage me from buying a pre-88.  I like the feel of the steering a little better.  I feel its easier to get the 88 to take a line thru a turn than the 85.  The 88 is also a lot less harsh over bumps and expansion joints.  The new suspension managed to allow a nicer ride without sacraficing body lean!  <P>The brakes are the biggest improvement!  Hands down the 88 brakes just flat out work better.  The down side, it took me the better part of two weeks to replace the rears.  Took me forever to take them apart (oh, so you pound the pins out from the back, not the front!)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Then I got everything re-assembled with new pads and the caliers leaked so apart again... and then the wait for rebuild kits to show up.  But I have had problems with brake repairs like this on other cars too (except those lousy spring-pins!)||||reg||1
Z||000012||AkursedX||11-21-2001||01:08 PM||||Well this is comparing apples and oranges, but my 88GT handles MUCH better than my 87GT.<P>BUT, my 88 is lowered with KYB's, poly everything, and it rides on 215/45-17, and 235/45-17 Kuhmos.  This car handles better than anything I have ever driven.  My 87 on the otherhand, stock springs, rubber bushings, all new ball joints and steering dampner, KYB front shocks, and gabriel rear struts, and it rides on 195/75-14 winter tires handles like my mothers malibu. The understeer is awful, and it has a terrible amount of body roll.  <P>But these really can't compare because it's on 2 ends of the spectrum.  But even when my 88GT had it's stock suspension, I feel that it handled much better than my 87.  I believe most of it is from the tires though.  I will have to wait till spring to really tell what the difference is.  <P>On a positive side for the 87, it rides like a cadillac compared to my 88.<P>------------------<BR><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic2_2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||falconhulk||11-21-2001||01:14 PM||||I have a 85 se v6 and it is the best handleing car I have ever drove.  This includes my firebird.  I keep reading about this terrible understeer.  I have not experienced this at all.  I corner at like 2 times the speed limit or more on windy mountain roads.  Never had one problem.  Never even had the tires skid sideways.  ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Mr.Frosty||11-21-2001||02:07 PM||||I used to own 2 '88 Formulas.The only difference between it and the '85 GT was the steering felt precise on the '88 and didn't wander on the highway,but that may be the steering damper which I'm going to replace soon.As far as handling goes,the '88 was a little better than the GT,but not much.The threshold is the same,but the Formula was more composed.The Gt is also lowered 2" all around and has a ST sway bar on the back.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT Turbo intercooled||||reg||1
Z||000015||Brian87egotrip||11-22-2001||11:55 AM||||I have an 87 gt.  I lowered it put on kyb's. Added a Held motorsports rear sway bar. Handles great .  Then I put on the fiero store front sway bar. OMG it handles like a dream. I cannot get it to break away from me at all. And believe me I do try. Rides rough over the bumps but I wanted corning ablity. As far as the brakes they did suck but I convereted them to the grand am.  Front only, much better. Now if I could get that engine to crank out more power.  Ahhh dreams.<P>------------------<BR>Brian                    87GT||||reg||1
Z||000016||jubjub2m6||11-22-2001||03:20 PM||||Ya I dont know why everyone rags on the 84-87 suspension...my 86 will most likely outstop a 88GT any day of the week and most likely out corner it also......... well I guess it doesnt help that I have 10.5" vented crossdrilled rotors with the new brake master cyl meaty tires and a beefy front sway bar and rear swaybar..=) ||||reg||14
Z||000017||artherd||11-22-2001||10:50 PM||||Much of it has to do with the undersized and economey rooling stock that the cars came with Stock.<P>When both cars are set up properly (ie, just replace all wearables with new stock stuff, and throw on a set of 17s) the 84-87 does handle reasonably well.  Over smooth pavement. (it has serious rear bump-steer issues, as well as a pronounced front lift-and-dive under accell/brake (off-center shocks.)<P>The 88 on the other hand, seems to get better and better the furthur you push it.  You can slide the car all the way to the limit, kick the tail out, catch it, then modulate the slide all the way through a corner.<P>It's not so much that the *design* of the 87-84 suspension is bad (though they are often in a poorly maintained *condition...*)<P>It's just that the 88 setup is so much better.<P>You think a N/A 911 is pretty nice, untill you drive a 360 Modena...<P>(note that neither car exactly sucks...)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by AutoXdbdragSE:<BR><B>Why is it that everyone rags on the 84-87 suspension.. it's not that bad.. i've yet to ride in an 88 fiero so i'm not sure how "MUCH BETTER" they are.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That... could have somethig to do with it, doncha think?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000018||SLYGUY||11-22-2001||11:34 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Master_Sushi:<BR><B> When I would push my 88 really hard the whole car seems to slide evenly.<P>When hiting bumps on the road in an 88 it is way more stable, at high speeds much beter also. I beleave the 88s are just a more modern and updated/upgraded setup. Also the brakes are WAY better.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I agree. I had an 86SEV6 back in 1993 and I didn't know that the 88s had better suspension. Its been eight years between fieros for me and I noticed the difference right away. I remember the front of my 86 skipping and sliding a little in sharp corners I went way too fast into but my 88 just positively STICKS.<P>Reminds me of a go-cart every time I rail in it. No body roll (my car is lowered in front), no sliding, no squealing. This is the first car that actually scared me a little at first because it handles SO well!!!<P>Sly<P><p>[This message has been edited by SLYGUY (edited 11-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||Posthumane||11-22-2001||11:37 PM||||Took my car to a rally cross the other day. Understeered like crazy if you tried to push it thru a corner, really hard to drive fast on loose stuff. I don't think that's a problem that was solved on 88's tho, just doesn't have the weight in the front to be easy on gravel. I love the handling of my 84 on pavement tho. I can usually get a nice drift going by a quick lift-turn-gas approach. Much better than the FWDs i've driven.||||reg||1
Z||000020||mrfiero||11-22-2001||11:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fformula88:<BR><B>Took me forever to take them apart (oh, so you pound the pins out from the back, not the front!)   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>You should never have to remove the spring pins on '88 calipers!  There is just no reason to mess with them.....the '88 caliper is a 2-piece design, so just split the caliper, remove the outer pad, remove the rotor then remove the inner pad.  The spring pins stay attached to the caliper body!  If you then need to remove the main body (like to rebuild it) then just unbolt it from the spindle.....the spring pins never need to come out.  <P><BR>||||reg||1
